import { makeArticle as a, section as s, type Article } from "@/lib/article-model";

export const roadTripArticles: Article[] = [
  a({slug:"coromandel-rotorua-road-trip",title:"Auckland, Coromandel and Rotorua: a 5-day camping loop",description:"Pacific beaches, forest roads and geothermal country on a compact North Island circuit.",category:"Road trips",region:"Upper North Island",places:["Auckland","Thames","Coromandel Town","Whitianga","Rotorua"],imageAlt:"Coromandel coast seen from a winding road",intro:"Five days is enough to connect the Coromandel Peninsula with Rotorua when the first and last drives stay disciplined. The reward is a route that moves from salt air to native forest and geothermal steam without wasting a day in transit.",sections:[s("Day 1 and 2: into the Coromandel","Leave Auckland outside commuter traffic and use Thames to buy food and fuel before the coast road narrows. The western side is beautiful but slower than the distance suggests, especially for a tall campervan.","Spend the second day around Whangamatā, Hot Water Beach or Cathedral Cove according to current access. Choose a legal campground near the activity rather than crossing the peninsula after sunset."),s("Day 3: cross to the forest","Use Whitianga or Coromandel Town for a proper reset before the inland drive. Kauaeranga Valley camps suit travellers ready for simpler facilities, while holiday parks offer showers, power and an easier wet-weather night.","The peninsula receives heavy rain and slips can affect local roads. Check Waka Kotahi and council updates before committing to a remote approach, then keep a sealed-road alternative saved."),s("Day 4 and 5: Rotorua and return","Rotorua needs a full day for geothermal walks, lakes and cultural experiences. Stay in an authorised campground or holiday park and treat geothermal fences and warning signs as firm boundaries.","Return to Auckland through Waikato or continue south if this loop opens a longer trip. Do not pair a late thermal-pool session with a tired drive through unfamiliar rural roads."),s("Roads that take longer than the map says","The 309 Road across the Coromandel range is unsealed in part and slow, and the coastal route between Thames and Coromandel Town is a narrow shelf road with almost no passing opportunity. Neither is difficult, but both run well over the time a routing app suggests, particularly in a large vehicle.","Allow a full unhurried day for the peninsula rather than treating it as a transit. The reward is the beaches on the east side, and rushing past them to reach Rotorua on schedule removes the point of the loop.",["309 Road is partly unsealed","Thames to Coromandel is narrow and slow","Give the peninsula a whole day"]),s("Cathedral Cove and Hot Water Beach need timing","Hot Water Beach only works around two hours either side of low tide, and the tide table decides your day rather than your itinerary. Check it before you set the order of stops, because arriving at high tide means there is genuinely nothing to do there.","Cathedral Cove access has changed repeatedly after storm damage and slips, and the walking route and parking arrangements have been altered more than once. Check the current DOC status rather than an older blog, and consider the water taxi from Hahei if the track is restricted."),s("Booking the summer beaches","Between Christmas and late January the east coast campgrounds at Hahei, Hot Water Beach and Ōpoutere fill completely, often booked months ahead. Arriving without a reservation in that window is not a plan, and the freedom camping options on the peninsula are limited and heavily patrolled.","Outside that window the same sites have space a few days out and cost less. If your dates are flexible at all, February and March give you the same beaches without the queue.",["Peak beaches book months ahead","Freedom camping is limited here","February and March are far easier"]),s("Rotorua earns a slower stop","The geothermal parks, the redwoods and the lake all deserve more than an afternoon, and the holiday parks here are genuinely convenient with the added novelty of thermally heated pools. It is a good place to spend two nights rather than one, particularly if the Coromandel weather has been wet.","The sulphur smell is real and fades from your awareness within a few hours. What does not fade is the effect on silver jewellery, so leave anything precious packed for the stay."),{heading:"Choosing where to base each night",body:["Thames works as a first night if you leave Auckland late, but it is a service town rather than a destination and the peninsula proper starts north of it. Whitianga and Hahei are the natural east coast bases, with Hahei closest to the walks and Whitianga carrying more services and a wider choice of campgrounds.","On the Rotorua leg, the lakeside holiday parks put you close to the geothermal parks and the redwoods without needing to drive back into town each morning. Blue Lake and Lake Ōkāreka are quieter than the central options and only a short drive from the attractions.","If the weather turns on the peninsula, the sensible move is to cut a beach day and arrive in Rotorua early. Almost everything there works in rain, which is rarely true of the Coromandel coast."]},s("Getting out of Auckland","Leaving Auckland southbound on a Friday afternoon or returning on a Sunday evening adds a long time to the drive. Aim to travel against the flow, or leave early enough that the motorway is still moving.","The Hauraki Rail Trail out of Thames is a flat cycle route through the plains and gorge, and bike hire is easy. It works well as a rest day if the peninsula weather is poor.",["Avoid Friday and Sunday motorway peaks","Hauraki Rail Trail is a good wet-day option"])]}),
  a({slug:"northland-cape-reinga-camping-route",title:"Northland to Cape Reinga: a 7-day camping route",description:"Harbours, kauri forest and long northern beaches on the road to Te Rerenga Wairua.",category:"Road trips",region:"Northland",places:["Whangārei","Paihia","Matauri Bay","Ahipara","Cape Reinga"],imageAlt:"Northland coastline and headland",intro:"The road north is not only a dash to the lighthouse. A week allows time for the Bay of Islands, west-coast forest and the cultural weight of Te Rerenga Wairua, with nights placed where services naturally thin out.",sections:[s("Whangārei to the Bay of Islands","Break the first leg around Whangārei Heads or Tutukaka before reaching Paihia. Holiday parks are convenient around the bay, while smaller coastal camps need closer checks for access, water and booking rules.","Use the passenger ferry to Russell rather than shifting the vehicle for every outing. Waitangi Treaty Grounds deserves unhurried time and should be approached as a living historic place, not a quick photo stop."),s("Across to the west coast","Travel through Kerikeri and Kāeo toward Ahipara, or cross to Waipoua Forest if the route and conditions allow. Follow every kauri hygiene station and keep footwear free of soil between forest walks.","Ninety Mile Beach is officially a road, but it is not an ordinary road. Tides, stream entrances, rental exclusions and soft sand make a coach trip or local advice the sensible choice for many visitors."),s("Cape Reinga and the final night","Carry enough fuel for the northern leg and check campsite details before reception disappears. Te Paki and nearby DOC options are exposed, simple and much better approached before dark.","At Cape Reinga, stay on formed paths and respect the significance of the headland to Māori. Leave the return drive for daylight and avoid turning the lighthouse visit into a race back to Auckland."),s("Ninety Mile Beach is not a road for your vehicle","The beach is legally a road, and it is also where a steady stream of rental vehicles are written off every year by incoming tide and soft sand. Almost every rental agreement prohibits it, insurance will not cover the recovery, and a stuck vehicle on a rising tide is lost quickly.","Take a commercial bus tour if you want to travel the beach. They run to tide windows, carry recovery equipment and know the entry points, and it costs a fraction of an excess claim.",["Rentals are not covered on the beach","Take the bus tour instead","Tide takes vehicles every year"]),s("Fuel and supplies thin out north of Kaitaia","Kaitaia is the last town with full services before Cape Reinga, and the stretch beyond it has limited fuel, few shops and patchy reception. Fill up and restock there rather than assuming something will appear, and check opening hours if you are travelling on a Sunday.","The road to the Cape is sealed the whole way now, which surprises people expecting gravel, but it is still a long out-and-back. Budget most of a day for the return trip from a base around Ahipara or Pukenui."),s("Respect the significance of Te Rerenga Wairua","Cape Reinga is one of the most spiritually significant places in the country for Māori, the departure point of spirits on their journey. Food is not permitted at the lighthouse area, and the expectation of quiet respectful behaviour is not a tourism formality.","Read the signage at the car park before walking down. Observing what is asked costs nothing and matters a great deal to the people whose place it is.",["No food at the lighthouse area","Read the signage before walking down","Quiet respect is expected"]),s("Kauri dieback governs the forest walks","Waipoua Forest and the northern kauri stands are managed under strict hygiene controls because kauri dieback is spread by soil on footwear. Use every cleaning station, scrub the soil off properly rather than just spraying, and stay on the boardwalks without exception.","Some tracks are closed permanently to protect stands and will not reopen. Check the current DOC status before planning a walk around a specific tree, because the situation changes and old guidance circulates widely."),{heading:"Both coasts are worth doing",body:["Northland has two very different coastlines and the standard mistake is running north on one and straight back down the same road. The east coast has the sheltered bays, the swimming and most of the towns; the west has Ninety Mile Beach, the giant dunes at Te Paki and the kauri forests.","A clean route runs north up the east coast through Whangārei, Paihia and Matauri Bay, out to the Cape, then south down the west through Ahipara, the Hokianga and Waipoua Forest. It costs no extra driving and gives you two entirely different weeks in one.","Sandboarding at Te Paki is genuinely good fun and boards can be hired at the dunes. Wear something you do not mind ruining, and walk up the firm sand at the edge rather than the middle, which is exhausting."]},s("The Hokianga and the kauri forests","The southern half of the west coast route runs through the Hokianga, with the harbour crossing at Rawene by vehicle ferry and the giant dunes at Opononi opposite. It is quieter and more atmospheric than the east coast towns.","Waipoua Forest sits just south of there and holds Tane Mahuta. Allow time for the hygiene stations and note that photography stops and parking are limited, so an early or late visit is considerably better.",["Rawene vehicle ferry saves a long drive","Visit Waipoua early or late"])]}),
  a({slug:"bay-of-islands-camping-week",title:"A week camping around the Bay of Islands",description:"Island ferries, sheltered coves and historic waterfront towns without changing camp every night.",category:"Road trips",region:"Bay of Islands",places:["Paihia","Waitangi","Russell","Kerikeri","Urupukapuka Island"],imageAlt:"Boats in a calm Bay of Islands cove",intro:"The Bay of Islands works best from two bases rather than seven different camps. Keep the vehicle still for a few days, use ferries and boats for the water, then shift north for Kerikeri and the quieter bays.",sections:[s("Choose the first base","Paihia keeps ferries, groceries and tours within walking distance, while Russell offers a smaller waterfront atmosphere after the day visitors leave. Confirm parking and late-arrival conditions before choosing between them.","Use the first morning for Waitangi and allow more time than the headline attractions suggest. The grounds, museum and landscape explain far more when the visit is not squeezed between boat departures."),s("Let the boat replace the road","Sailing, kayaking and water taxis reveal why this region is a bay rather than a chain of viewpoints. Check marine weather and operator conditions, then pack sun protection and a warm layer even on a bright morning.","Urupukapuka and other islands have conservation rules that protect wildlife. Clean gear, take rubbish away and use only the campsites and facilities identified for overnight stays."),s("Kerikeri and the northern bays","Move to Kerikeri for food, orchards, historic sites and easier access to Matauri Bay or Whangaroa. Summer bookings and local road traffic can make a spontaneous coastal night difficult.","Leave one day unassigned. Wind may move a boat trip, rain may favour a forest walk, and a flexible plan gives the best bay of the week room to become a second visit."),s("Getting out on the water is the point","The Bay of Islands is a marine destination and a week spent entirely on land misses most of it. Options run from the passenger ferry between Paihia and Russell, which takes minutes and costs little, through to full-day sailing, dolphin and Hole in the Rock trips.","Book weather-dependent trips early in your stay rather than late. That way a cancelled day can be rescheduled within the week instead of becoming the thing you missed.",["Book boat trips early in the week","The Russell ferry is cheap and quick","Leave a spare day for weather"]),s("Urupukapuka Island camping","DOC runs campsites on Urupukapuka, reached by water taxi from Paihia or Russell. It is a genuinely different experience from a mainland holiday park: no vehicles, limited facilities, and you carry everything in and out including your rubbish.","Book the site and the boat together and check the return timetable carefully, because the last taxi is not negotiable. Take more water than you expect to need, since supply on the island is limited and untreated."),s("Waitangi is worth the entry fee","The Waitangi Treaty Grounds are the single most historically important site in the country, and the guided tour and cultural performance are what make the visit land rather than the buildings alone. Allow half a day, and take the guided option rather than wandering it alone.","For a fuller picture, pair it with the Russell museum and Christ Church, which carries musket ball damage from the 1845 fighting. The two sites together explain the region far better than either does on its own.",["Take the guided tour, not self-guided","Allow half a day at Waitangi","Pair it with Russell for context"]),s("Summer here is genuinely busy","January in Paihia and Russell means full campgrounds, booked boat trips and queues for the ferry. It is also warm, calm and exactly when the water is best, so the trade is real rather than one-sided.","Shoulder season in November or March gives you the same coastline with space and lower prices, though the water is cooler and some operators reduce their schedules. Check that the specific trip you want is still running."),{heading:"Where to stay around the bay",body:["Paihia has the most accommodation and the ferry to Russell, which makes it the practical base for a week without a boat. It is also the busiest and noisiest, particularly in January. Russell is quieter and more attractive but has fewer campgrounds and everything runs on the ferry timetable.","Kerikeri sits inland with the historic Stone Store and Kemp House, good food and cheaper sites, and it works well for a couple of nights if you want a break from the waterfront. It is around twenty minutes from Paihia.","For something quieter again, the campgrounds around Matauri Bay and Tauranga Bay north of the Bay of Islands give you comparable coastline with a fraction of the traffic, and they are an easy detour on the way north."]},s("Rainy day options","Northland gets substantial rain and a week here will usually include a wet day. The Waitangi Treaty Grounds, the Kerikeri historic buildings and the Kawiti glow worm caves near Waiomio all work regardless of weather.","Boat trips are the thing most likely to be cancelled, which is the argument for booking them early in the week rather than saving them for the end.",["Book boat trips early in the week","Waitangi and Kerikeri work in rain"])]}),
  a({slug:"east-cape-road-trip",title:"East Cape road trip: Gisborne to Ōpōtiki",description:"A slow coastal circuit through surf towns, pōhutukawa bays and remote eastern roads.",category:"Road trips",region:"East Cape",places:["Gisborne","Tolaga Bay","Tokomaru Bay","Te Araroa","Ōpōtiki"],imageAlt:"Remote East Cape road beside the Pacific",intro:"State Highway 35 traces one of the country's most rewarding slow roads. Fuel, opening hours and storm damage matter more here, so the itinerary should follow daylight and local conditions instead of a fixed arrival time.",sections:[s("Gisborne to Tolaga Bay","Start with a full tank and groceries in Gisborne, then stop at coastal settlements without assuming every service will be open. Tolaga Bay gives the first night an easy landmark and a chance to walk rather than drive.","Ask permission wherever land or beach access is not clearly public. Marae, urupā and working farms are not informal camping grounds, even when an old online comment suggests otherwise."),s("Around the cape","Tokomaru Bay and Te Araroa reward travellers who stay long enough to notice the communities between viewpoints. Drive conservatively through narrow bends and expect livestock, cyclists and road repairs.","The side trip to the East Cape lighthouse includes a long staircase and remote access. Check the road before setting out and do not count on reception for a last-minute campsite search."),s("Finish through Waihau Bay","Waihau Bay and the coast toward Ōpōtiki provide a gentler final leg when weather is settled. Storms can close or damage the highway, so review council and Waka Kotahi notices each morning.","Reach Ōpōtiki with daylight for fuel, supplies and the next decision. If the road has demanded more concentration than expected, stop there instead of continuing tired toward Tauranga or Rotorua."),s("This is a route about slowing down","State Highway 35 around the Cape is one of the few places left in New Zealand where you can drive for an hour and pass almost nobody. There is no headline attraction, and travellers who arrive looking for one are usually disappointed. The road, the bays and the pace are the point.","Give it three days minimum. Done as a two-day dash between Gisborne and Ōpōtiki it becomes a long drive through places you did not stop at, which is the one way to get this route wrong.",["Three days minimum for the Cape","There is no headline attraction","Passing nobody is the appeal"]),s("Services and reception are genuinely sparse","Fuel is available at intervals but not everywhere, opening hours are short, and card facilities occasionally fail. Carry cash, fill up at Gisborne and Ōpōtiki, and top up whenever you pass a station with more than half a tank showing.","Mobile coverage disappears for long stretches. Download what you need, tell someone your rough plan, and do not count on being able to look something up or call ahead from the road."),s("You are travelling through iwi land and small communities","The Cape is predominantly Māori land and the communities along it are small and close. Freedom camping is restricted in many places and the local expectation of respectful behaviour is high, particularly around marae, urupā and access to beaches.","Ask rather than assume, buy from the local shops, and never camp somewhere simply because nobody appeared to stop you. The welcome here is real and it depends on visitors behaving well.",["Ask before camping anywhere unclear","Buy from the local shops","Marae and urupā are not attractions"]),s("East Cape Lighthouse and the first sunrise","The lighthouse walk is roughly 700 steps from the car park at the end of a gravel road, and the reward is the first place on mainland New Zealand to see the sunrise. It is a genuinely worthwhile early start, particularly around the solstice.","The access road is unsealed and can be rough after rain. Check its condition and your rental agreement before committing, and allow considerably longer than the distance suggests."),{heading:"What is actually worth stopping for",body:["Tolaga Bay wharf is the longest in the country at over 600 metres and worth the walk to the end. Tokomaru Bay has the ruins of the old freezing works and a genuinely good beach. Te Puia Springs and Waipiro Bay are small and quiet in a way that rewards an unplanned stop.","Tikitiki has St Mary's Church, one of the most remarkable interiors in New Zealand, decorated throughout with carving and tukutuku and built as a memorial to Ngāti Porou soldiers. It is unlocked and free and most people drive past it without knowing.","Hicks Bay and Te Araroa mark the northern end, and the pōhutukawa at Te Araroa is claimed to be the largest in the country. From there it is a short run to the lighthouse road and then west towards Ōpōtiki."]},s("Timing and season","Summer brings the pohutukawa into flower along the coast, which is the classic image of this route, and the small settlements are busiest over the Christmas period. Outside that the road is close to empty.","Winter is quiet and green but services reduce further and daylight is short for a route where the driving is the point. Autumn is the sweet spot for both weather and space.",["Pohutukawa flower in December","Autumn balances weather and quiet"])]}),
  a({slug:"taranaki-forgotten-world-highway",title:"Taranaki and the Forgotten World Highway camping loop",description:"Black-sand coast, volcanic forest and the remote road between Stratford and Taumarunui.",category:"Road trips",region:"Taranaki and King Country",places:["New Plymouth","Egmont National Park","Stratford","Whangamōmona","Taumarunui"],imageAlt:"Mount Taranaki above a rural highway",intro:"This loop circles a mountain, then follows State Highway 43 through saddles, farmland and the single-lane Moki Tunnel. Three or four nights creates space for weather around Taranaki and a patient crossing of the Forgotten World Highway.",sections:[s("New Plymouth and the mountain","Use New Plymouth as the reliable base for fuel, food and a coastal day. Mountain cloud changes quickly, so walk the lower tracks when visibility or wind makes exposed routes a poor choice.","Egmont National Park trailheads are not interchangeable campsites. Stay only where overnight use is permitted and use the exact access advice for the track and vehicle."),s("Stratford to Whangamōmona","Fill the tank in Stratford and treat the highway as the day's experience. The sealed and unsealed sections, narrow bridges and tight bends suit a calm pace rather than a target arrival time.","Whangamōmona has limited accommodation and camping capacity. Confirm the night in advance, particularly around events, and carry food for hours when small businesses are closed."),s("Taumarunui and the return","The Tangarakau Gorge and Moki Tunnel demand attention from wide or high vehicles. Know the campervan dimensions and stop before any section that does not provide safe clearance.","Taumarunui restores ordinary services and opens routes toward Tongariro or Waikato. Check fatigue before adding another long leg, because the highway's short distance hides a full day of concentration."),s("The Forgotten World Highway is a commitment","State Highway 43 runs roughly 150 kilometres between Stratford and Taumarunui with no fuel along it, a section of unsealed road through the Tangarakau Gorge, and a single-lane tunnel. It takes far longer than the distance implies and there is no way to shortcut out of the middle.","Fill the tank at either end, carry water and food, and check the road status before setting out. It is a fine drive in reasonable weather and an unpleasant one in heavy rain.",["No fuel for 150 kilometres","Part unsealed, one single-lane tunnel","Check road status before you start"]),s("Whangamōmona is the reason to go slowly","The self-declared Republic of Whangamōmona, with its own passports stamped at the hotel, is the highlight of the route and a genuinely good pub stop. The Republic Day celebration runs biennially and fills the village completely, which is either the reason to go or the reason to avoid those dates.","There is a small camping area and the hotel has rooms. Either is a better plan than trying to drive the whole highway and reach a town at the far end in one day."),s("Mount Taranaki is a serious mountain","The Pouākai Crossing and the summit route look approachable from the road and are not. Taranaki has a high fatality record, weather that changes within an hour, and terrain that becomes genuinely dangerous in cloud. It is not a mountain to attempt casually.","Check the forecast for the mountain rather than New Plymouth, tell someone your plan, and turn back early. The lower walks around Dawson Falls and the Kamahi loop give you the forest and the views without the exposure.",["Forecast the mountain, not the town","Taranaki weather turns within the hour","Lower walks give the views safely"]),s("New Plymouth is underrated as a base","The Coastal Walkway runs 13 kilometres along the waterfront, the Len Lye Centre is genuinely good, and the city has the services to reset a trip properly before or after the highway. It works far better as a two-night base than a single overnight.","Use it to do laundry, restock and check the mountain forecast before deciding which of the Egmont walks to attempt. The weather often makes that decision for you."),{heading:"The Surf Highway and the coast",body:["State Highway 45 loops around the western side of the mountain between New Plymouth and Hāwera, and it is a far easier drive than the Forgotten World Highway with a string of surf beaches along it. Ōakura, Ōpunake and Kina Beach all have camping and the coastline is consistently good.","The contrast is the point of a Taranaki loop: the mountain in the middle, forest and farmland on the eastern side, and a run of black sand surf beaches on the west. Doing both sides makes it a proper circuit rather than an out-and-back.","Cape Egmont lighthouse and the coastal road around Pungarehu give the classic view of the mountain rising straight out of farmland. It is best in the early morning before cloud forms on the summit, which it usually does by late morning."]},s("Rail carts and the tunnel","Operators run rail carts along the disused Stratford to Okahukura line, including through the Hobbits Hole tunnel, which is a genuinely unusual way to see the country the highway passes through.","These need booking ahead and run to a schedule rather than on demand. If it appeals, build the day around the departure time rather than hoping to fit it in.",["Rail carts need booking ahead","Build the day around the departure"])]}),
  a({slug:"wellington-wairarapa-camping-loop",title:"Wellington and Wairarapa: a 4-day camping loop",description:"Harbour hills, vineyard towns and an exposed southern coast within a short circuit.",category:"Road trips",region:"Lower North Island",places:["Wellington","Lower Hutt","Martinborough","Cape Palliser","Greytown"],imageAlt:"Wairarapa coast and lighthouse road",intro:"Four days can move from Wellington's compact centre to Wairarapa wine country and the wild coast at Cape Palliser. The distances are moderate, but wind and the Remutaka road decide how relaxed they feel.",sections:[s("Cross the Remutaka Range","Leave Wellington after the commuter peak and take State Highway 2 toward Featherston. Strong wind, rain and traffic can make the crossing tiring in a high-sided campervan, so slow down or delay rather than fighting the vehicle.","Martinborough and Greytown make practical bases with food, powered sites and short local drives. If wine is part of the day, walk, cycle with care or use a tour instead of moving the vehicle."),s("Cape Palliser day","The road past Lake Ferry narrows and runs close to the sea before Cape Palliser. Check swell, wind and recent road conditions, then leave enough daylight for the return.","Keep distance from fur seals and never block the road or boat access around Ngawi. The lighthouse stairs are steep, and the coast offers little shelter when a southerly arrives."),s("Return through the towns","Use the final morning for the smaller Wairarapa towns or a walk around the wetlands, then cross the range before fatigue and evening traffic combine.","A Wellington-area holiday park can be the smartest last night before a ferry. It gives laundry, power and a predictable departure without adding a city-centre parking problem."),s("The Rimutaka Hill Road sets the tone","The road between Upper Hutt and Featherston climbs steeply with tight corners and is exposed to the wind that Wellington is known for. It is fine in a car and demands real attention in a campervan or with a trailer, particularly in a northerly.","Check the road status before crossing, because it closes to high-sided vehicles in strong wind and occasionally altogether. The alternative routes are long, so a closure changes the whole day rather than adding twenty minutes.",["Closes to high-sided vehicles in wind","Steep with tight corners","Detour routes are long"]),s("Cape Palliser is the best day on the loop","The drive out past Ngawi to the lighthouse takes you along an exposed coast lined with bulldozers used to launch fishing boats, past the largest fur seal colony on the North Island, to 250 steps up to the lighthouse. It feels considerably more remote than its distance from Wellington.","Keep well back from the seals, particularly between pups and the water, and never get between an animal and its escape route to the sea. They move faster than they look and will defend the space."),s("Martinborough works on a bicycle","The wineries sit in a compact grid within easy riding distance of the village square, which makes cycling between them the standard and sensible way to do a tasting day. Bike hire is available in the village and removes the drink-driving question entirely.","Book a table for lunch somewhere in advance on a weekend, because the village is small and fills. Most cellar doors charge a tasting fee that is credited against a purchase.",["Hire bikes, do not drive between wineries","Book weekend lunch ahead","Tasting fees credit against purchases"]),s("Wellington itself needs a plan for the vehicle","Parking a campervan in central Wellington is difficult and expensive, and the height restrictions catch people out in the central car parks. Base yourself in the Hutt Valley or at a coastal campground and use the train or bus into the city.","Te Papa is free and genuinely world class, and the cable car and Zealandia are both worth a half day. None of that requires the van to be anywhere near the centre."),{heading:"Putangirua Pinnacles and the coast road",body:["The Pinnacles are a badlands formation of eroded gravel spires reached by a walk up a streambed, and they were used as the Paths of the Dead in the film trilogy. The valley route is a scramble over loose rock; the ridge track above gives the overview and takes longer.","The road out to Cape Palliser beyond the Pinnacles is unsealed in places and can be affected by slips and by the sea in a big swell. Check conditions before committing, particularly in a large vehicle, because turning around is difficult.","Ngawi, the fishing village with a line of bulldozers on the beach, is the most photographed part of this coast and a good place to stop. There are no services beyond it, so fuel and food come from Martinborough or Featherston."]},s("Zealandia and the city wildlife","Zealandia is a fenced urban sanctuary where kaka, takahe, tuatara and tui are common and visible, and the night tour offers a realistic chance of seeing kiwi close to the city centre.","It books out, particularly the night tour, and it is a much better wildlife experience than most people expect from something inside a capital city.",["Night tour offers a kiwi chance","Book the night tour ahead"])]}),
  a({slug:"north-island-two-week-road-trip",title:"North Island camping road trip: a practical 14-day route",description:"Two weeks from Auckland through volcanic country, the capital and the western coast.",category:"Road trips",region:"North Island",places:["Auckland","Coromandel","Rotorua","Tongariro","Wellington","Taranaki"],imageAlt:"Campervan route through the central North Island",intro:"A fortnight cannot cover every corner of Te Ika-a-Māui well. This route chooses a coherent loop, protects two-night stays in the places that need them and leaves Northland or East Cape for another journey.",sections:[s("Auckland to volcanic country","Spend the first night outside Auckland traffic, then give Coromandel and Rotorua two nights each. This creates time for a beach or forest day and a geothermal day without packing camp every morning.","Use Taupō to restock before Tongariro. Alpine plans must follow the forecast, transport arrangements and volcanic or track alerts, with a lower walk ready when conditions are wrong."),s("South to Wellington","Break the central drive around Whanganui or the Kāpiti Coast. Wellington rewards a parked vehicle, and a campground near public transport avoids height limits and expensive central parking.","If a Cook Strait ferry follows, check in according to the vehicle booking and allow for wind disruption. Keep medicines, warm layers and essential documents outside any vehicle deck you cannot access underway."),s("Return through Taranaki","Travel north through the Wairarapa only if time allows; otherwise use the west coast toward Taranaki. New Plymouth provides an easy two-night base for coast and mountain options.","Finish through Waikato with the final night close enough to Auckland for a calm return. Cleaning, fuel and rental handover take longer than the last line on the itinerary suggests."),s("Fourteen days means choosing a side","The common mistake is trying to include Northland, the Coromandel, the central plateau, the East Cape and Taranaki in one fortnight. That produces a driving holiday with no time in any of it. Pick either the northern loop or the eastern and central route and do it properly.","A workable shape is Auckland, Coromandel, Rotorua, Taupō, Tongariro, Napier, Wellington, with two nights in at least two of those. Northland then becomes a separate trip rather than a rushed appendage.",["Do not try to fit Northland in as well","Two nights in at least two places","Choose one loop and do it properly"]),s("Tongariro Alpine Crossing needs a real plan","It is a 19.4 kilometre one-way alpine route, requires shuttle transport at both ends, and there is a parking time limit at Mangatepopo that effectively forces the shuttle. It is also frequently closed or unsafe in wind, cloud or ice, and people get into serious trouble every year.","Book the shuttle, check the DOC forecast on the morning, carry warm and waterproof layers regardless of the sunshine at the car park, and be genuinely willing to abandon it. Leave a spare day in the itinerary so a cancellation is not the end of it."),s("Geothermal country rewards choosing carefully","Rotorua and Taupō have more geothermal attractions than anyone needs, and they are not interchangeable. Wai-O-Tapu, Waimangu, Orakei Korako and Craters of the Moon each offer something different and pricing varies widely, with one of them free.","Pick one paid park and one free thermal area rather than three paid ones. The novelty wears off faster than the entry fees suggest, and the money is better spent on a lake trip or a day walk.",["One paid park is usually enough","They are not interchangeable","Craters of the Moon is the cheap option"]),s("Leave the last day soft","If you are flying out of Auckland or catching a ferry from Wellington, do not plan to arrive on the morning of departure. Roadworks, weather and a slow last day are all normal, and the North Island's main routes carry heavy traffic around holidays.","Spend the final night within an hour of the terminal or airport. It converts a stressful morning into an ordinary one and costs nothing more than a slightly duller last campsite."),{heading:"Waitomo, Napier and the stops that get cut",body:["Waitomo's glowworm caves are a genuine highlight and sit awkwardly between Auckland and the central plateau, which is why they often get dropped. The black water rafting is more memorable than the standard walking tour if you have the time and inclination.","Napier's art deco centre is compact enough to see in a morning and pairs well with the Hawke's Bay wineries, most of which are within cycling distance of the city. It works best as a two-night stop rather than a pass-through.","If the fortnight is tight, Napier is the more defensible cut, because Waitomo has no substitute anywhere else on the route while wine regions appear again in Marlborough if you continue south."]},s("Where the driving days bite","Auckland to the Coromandel, and Taupo to Napier over the Titiokura, are the two legs most often underestimated. Both include winding sections that stretch well past their routing estimate.","Break either one with a stop rather than treating it as a transit. The alternative is arriving somewhere good with no energy left to enjoy it.",["Auckland to Coromandel runs long","Taupo to Napier is winding"])]}),
  a({slug:"picton-kaikoura-christchurch-route",title:"Picton to Christchurch via Kaikōura: a 4-day coastal route",description:"Sounds, vineyards, marine wildlife and a mountain-backed highway down the east coast.",category:"Road trips",region:"Marlborough and Canterbury",places:["Picton","Blenheim","Kaikōura","Waipara","Christchurch"],imageAlt:"Kaikōura coast with mountains behind the road",intro:"The direct drive is short enough for one day, but four days reveals a far richer coast. The plan gives Picton time to settle after the ferry, Kaikōura a full weather window and North Canterbury a final unhurried stop.",sections:[s("Picton and Marlborough","Do not rush straight from a late ferry onto the coastal highway. Use Picton for the first night, then explore the Sounds or travel through Blenheim after a proper rest.","Cellar doors require a sober driving plan and many operate by booking or seasonal hours. A campsite near Blenheim or Renwick lets the vehicle stay put while a tour handles transport."),s("Two nights in Kaikōura","State Highway 1 is exposed to weather, slips and occasional closures. Check the route before leaving Marlborough, then use the coastal pull-offs only where stopping is clearly safe.","Whale trips depend on sea conditions and may move or cancel. Two nights create a second opportunity, while the peninsula walkway, seal viewing and local food keep the spare day valuable."),s("North Canterbury finish","Waipara offers a slower break before Christchurch, with beaches and small towns nearby. Keep the final day light if city driving in a large vehicle is unfamiliar.","Choose a Christchurch campground based on the next task: airport, rental depot, city visit or inland departure. The best site is the one that removes an unnecessary crossing of the city."),s("The coast road has been rebuilt and still moves","State Highway 1 between Picton and Christchurch was severely damaged in the 2016 Kaikōura earthquake and has been substantially rebuilt, but the coastal section remains prone to slips and closures in heavy rain. Check the NZTA status before committing to it rather than assuming it is open.","When it closes, the inland route via the Lewis Pass is the alternative and adds several hours. That is worth knowing before you book something time-critical at either end.",["Check NZTA before the coast road","Slips still close it in heavy rain","Lewis Pass detour adds hours"]),s("Kaikōura is a booking, not a drive-through","Whale watching, dolphin swimming and albatross trips all run to weather and all book out in season. The whales are resident year-round, which makes Kaikōura unusually reliable, but the sea state cancels trips regularly enough that a single-night stop is a gamble.","Stay two nights and book the trip for the first morning. That gives the operator a second window to reschedule you into rather than refunding you as you drive away."),s("The seal colony and the peninsula walk","The colony at Point Kean is free, accessible and busy with fur seals, and the peninsula walkway above it is one of the better short coastal walks in the country. Keep twenty metres back from the animals and never block their path to the water.","The walkway is tide-dependent on the shore route and unaffected on the clifftop route. Check the tide before choosing which way round, or you will be turning back.",["Twenty metres from the seals","Shore route is tide-dependent","Clifftop route always works"]),s("Blenheim and the Waipara wineries","Marlborough around Blenheim is the country's largest wine region and most cellar doors are within a compact area, which makes a bicycle the sensible way to visit several. Waipara, closer to Christchurch, is smaller, quieter and often more interesting for a single stop.","If the itinerary only allows one wine stop, Waipara fits the route better on this drive and does not require a detour off the highway. Marlborough deserves its own day rather than a rushed afternoon."),{heading:"Queen Charlotte Drive and the Sounds",body:["The road from Picton to Havelock is only 35 kilometres and takes the better part of an hour, with continuous corners, narrow sections and viewpoints worth stopping at. It is the better alternative to the direct highway if you have the time, and a poor choice at dusk when tired.","Anakiwa at the end of the Queen Charlotte Track and the campsites around Momorangi Bay put you on the water and make a good first South Island night. Water taxis from Picton open up the walking track without committing to the full multi-day route.","If you are arriving on an evening sailing, stay in Picton rather than attempting this road in the dark. It is the kind of route where the scenery is the entire reason to take it."]},s("Where to break the drive","Picton to Christchurch is comfortably done in two days with a night in Kaikoura, and uncomfortably in one. The direct run is around five hours before any stops, on a road with plenty worth stopping for.","Waipara and the Hurunui are the natural second break if you have three days, with wineries and the Hanmer Springs detour inland.",["Two days beats one","Hanmer Springs is an easy detour"])]}),
  a({slug:"christchurch-tekapo-aoraki-road-trip",title:"Christchurch to Lake Tekapo and Aoraki: a 5-day road trip",description:"Canterbury plains, turquoise lakes and alpine weather on the classic inland route.",category:"Road trips",region:"Mackenzie Country",places:["Christchurch","Geraldine","Lake Tekapo","Twizel","Aoraki / Mount Cook"],imageAlt:"Road toward Aoraki beside Lake Pūkaki",intro:"Five days lets the Mackenzie Basin breathe. The journey is strongest when Tekapo and Aoraki each get a full day, with the weather deciding which walks and viewpoints belong on the schedule.",sections:[s("Cross the Canterbury Plains","Buy specialist food, fuel and equipment in Christchurch, then use Geraldine for a relaxed first break. Strong nor'west winds can affect high-sided vehicles even before the mountains appear.","Lake Tekapo fills quickly in peak seasons. Book the legal overnight stop instead of assuming an empty lakeside area permits camping, and protect the dark-sky environment by keeping light low."),s("Tekapo to Aoraki","Follow Lake Pūkaki north only after checking the Aoraki road and park alerts. The view encourages frequent stops, but use formed pull-offs and leave traffic lanes clear.","Aoraki weather changes rapidly and popular walks can close for wind, avalanche or flood risk. Start early, carry layers and choose a short valley walk when the alpine forecast turns."),s("Twizel and the return","Twizel provides fuel, groceries and a sheltered alternative to the exposed upper valley. It is also a sensible place to add a recovery night after a long hike.","Return through Fairlie or continue toward Wānaka only with a realistic driving day. The scenery does not make fatigue, crosswind or rental return times less real."),s("The Mackenzie is a dark sky reserve","The Aoraki Mackenzie International Dark Sky Reserve covers Tekapo, Twizel and the Aoraki area, and the night sky here is among the best accessible anywhere. Lighting is restricted throughout the reserve, which is why the stars are as good as they are.","Use a red torch, keep vehicle headlights off where you can, and give your eyes twenty minutes to adapt properly. A phone screen at full brightness undoes that for everyone around you.",["Use a red torch at night","Twenty minutes for eyes to adapt","Lighting is restricted for a reason"]),s("Tekapo is beautiful and extremely busy","The Church of the Good Shepherd is one of the most photographed buildings in the country and the coach traffic reflects that. Early morning and late evening are the only times it feels like the place in the photographs.","It is also an active church rather than a monument, and services take priority over photography. The lupins along the lakeshore, incidentally, are an invasive weed rather than a native feature, however good they look in November."),s("Aoraki weather and the Hooker Valley","The Hooker Valley Track is the standout short walk in the park: three hours return, largely flat, three swing bridges and a glacial lake at the end with the mountain above it. It is achievable for most people and worth timing for early light.","The weather at the village is not the weather in the valley, and wind funnels hard through it. Take a windproof layer even on a warm day, and check the forecast at the visitor centre rather than relying on a town forecast from Twizel.",["Hooker Valley is three hours return","Wind funnels hard through the valley","Check forecasts at the visitor centre"]),s("Accommodation is concentrated and books out","Aoraki village has limited beds and the DOC campsite at White Horse Hill fills daily in summer. Twizel and Lake Pukaki have more options and are within reasonable driving distance, which makes them the practical base for most travellers.","Book ahead in summer without exception, and note that there is no supermarket at the village. Restock in Twizel or Fairlie before heading up the valley."),{heading:"Geraldine, Fairlie and the approach",body:["The inland route through Geraldine and Fairlie is the standard approach and both towns are worth a stop for supplies, with Fairlie being the last practical restock before Tekapo. The climb over Burkes Pass marks the transition into the Mackenzie and the change in landscape is abrupt.","Lake Pukaki's viewpoint on the way to Aoraki gives the classic view up the lake to the mountain, and the colour is genuinely as unnatural as the photographs suggest, caused by suspended glacial flour. Early morning is calmest and the reflections are best before the wind gets up.","The road into Aoraki village is a dead end, 55 kilometres each way from the highway. Factor the return into the day rather than treating it as a stop on the way somewhere, because there is no through route."]},s("Hot pools and the wet-day option","Tekapo Springs and the pools at Omarama give you something to do when the weather closes in, which in the Mackenzie it does with little warning. Both are within easy reach of the main route.","The Mount John observatory tours are weather dependent and book out. If the forecast is clear on your first night, take it then rather than saving it.",["Take a clear night when you get it","Hot pools cover a bad weather day"])]}),
  a({slug:"west-coast-south-island-road-trip",title:"South Island West Coast camping road trip",description:"Rainforest, limestone, glaciers and the Tasman Sea from Westport to Haast.",category:"Road trips",region:"West Coast",places:["Westport","Punakaiki","Hokitika","Franz Josef","Fox Glacier","Haast"],imageAlt:"Rainforest meeting the Tasman Sea on the West Coast",intro:"The West Coast is a journey through weather rather than a checklist of sights. Six or seven days gives rain room to move, keeps glacier valleys from becoming rushed stops and places every long section inside daylight.",sections:[s("Westport to Hokitika","Check the Buller Gorge and coastal highway before leaving, then pause at Punakaiki around the tide and swell rather than a fixed minute. Blowholes are natural features, not scheduled performances.","Hokitika is the first strong resupply and a good place to dry gear. Buy pounamu only from reputable local sellers and treat cultural stories with more care than a souvenir label."),s("Glacier country","Franz Josef and Fox offer valley walks, weather-dependent flights and busy holiday accommodation. Current glacier access follows marked tracks and closures; old photos do not indicate where visitors can safely stand today.","Keep one unbooked activity window because cloud and rain move quickly. A wet day can still suit forest walks, hot pools or a slow drive, but never cross a barrier for a clearer view."),s("Haast and the alpine exit","The road south includes narrow bridges, waterfalls and sandfly country. Carry repellent, close screens early and use designated pull-offs instead of stopping on bends.","Before crossing Haast Pass, check for snow, slips and closures. Fuel in Haast or Fox and reach Wānaka before dark, because the final lakeside bends demand full attention."),s("Rain is the condition, not the exception","Parts of the West Coast receive over five metres of rain a year, and Fox and Franz Josef sit in some of the wettest inhabited country anywhere. A trip planned around avoiding rain here will fail; a trip planned around travelling in it works fine.","Pack for it properly: a genuinely waterproof shell, dry bags for everything that matters, spare socks, and somewhere in the vehicle to dry things. The reward is near-empty campsites and rainforest that looks the way it does because of the weather.",["Plan to travel in rain, not avoid it","Dry bags for anything that matters","Empty campsites are the payoff"]),s("The sandflies are not an exaggeration","West Coast sandflies are relentless at dawn and dusk, near water, and in still conditions. They will find any gap in the tent mesh and the bites itch for days. This single factor ruins more West Coast trips than the rain does.","Carry strong repellent and apply it before you get out of the vehicle rather than after. Cover ankles and wrists in the evening, check the tent mesh before you leave home, and pack antihistamine cream because you will want it."),s("The glaciers have retreated a long way","Franz Josef and Fox no longer have valley-floor access to the ice, and the terminal face is well beyond where the walking tracks end. The valley walks are still worth doing, but the only way onto the ice is a helicopter, which is expensive and weather-dependent.","Book a heli-hike early in your stay so a weather cancellation can be rescheduled. If it does not run, the Franz Josef valley walk and the Fox glacier viewpoint still give you the scale, and the Lake Matheson reflection walk is arguably the better experience.",["No valley-floor ice access any more","Heli-hikes cancel often for weather","Lake Matheson is the reliable alternative"]),s("Fuel, food and the Haast gap","The coast has long stretches between services, and the section from Fox through Haast to Wānaka is the one to plan for. Fill up before Haast, carry food, and expect no reception through much of it.","Punakaiki, Hokitika and Greymouth all have supplies and are worth stops in their own right. The pancake rocks are best at high tide with a swell running, which is worth checking rather than arriving at slack water on a calm day."),{heading:"Punakaiki, Hokitika and the stops between",body:["The Pancake Rocks at Punakaiki are best at high tide with a westerly swell running, when the blowholes work. At low tide on a calm day they are still interesting but considerably less dramatic, so check the tide before deciding when to stop.","Hokitika Gorge, about half an hour inland from the town, has water of a turquoise colour that looks manipulated in photographs and is not. It is a short easy walk and one of the better value stops on the coast.","Between the towns, the coast has a string of DOC sites and lagoons that reward an unplanned stop. Lake Mahinapua, Lake Ianthe and Ship Creek all have short walks and are far quieter than the glacier villages."]},s("Whitebait, greenstone and local rules","Whitebait season runs in spring with strict rules on method and location, and it is taken very seriously locally. Pounamu is legally owned by Ngai Tahu and taking greenstone from rivers and beaches is not permitted.","Buy pounamu from a licensed carver instead, which supports the people it belongs to and gets you a better piece than a river pebble.",["Do not take greenstone from rivers","Buy pounamu from a licensed carver"])]}),
  a({slug:"queenstown-glenorchy-camping",title:"Queenstown to Glenorchy: a 3-day camping escape",description:"A short road with enough lake, mountain and walking country to fill a long weekend.",category:"Road trips",region:"Whakatipu",places:["Queenstown","Twelve Mile Delta","Bob's Cove","Glenorchy","Paradise"],imageAlt:"Lake Whakatipu road near Glenorchy",intro:"The road to Glenorchy is only about 46 kilometres, but it deserves a full day. Three nights allow Queenstown supplies, a legal lakeside camp and one weather-dependent walk without turning every viewpoint into a hurried stop.",sections:[s("Leave Queenstown prepared","Fuel and shop before leaving town, then confirm the selected campground and its vehicle limits. Twelve Mile Delta is exposed and basic, while commercial options provide more certainty for power and showers.","Pull over only in formed bays on the Glenorchy road. Traffic, cyclists and blind corners make an improvised photo stop dangerous even when the lake appears empty."),s("Glenorchy day","Walk the lagoon boardwalk, visit the waterfront and choose a track that matches the forecast. Rees-Dart, Routeburn and Greenstone access involve different roads, bridges and backcountry preparation.","Glenorchy services are smaller than Queenstown's. Do not arrive expecting late fuel, groceries or an immediate mechanical repair, and carry cash or an alternative payment method."),s("Paradise or a slow return","Roads beyond Glenorchy can be unsealed, narrow and affected by fords or washouts. Rental conditions may prohibit the route, so stop where the vehicle agreement and current conditions stop.","Return with daylight and use Bob's Cove or another formed stop for the final walk. Let the lake road remain the experience instead of squeezing it between Queenstown bookings."),s("The Glenorchy road is the attraction","The 45 kilometres from Queenstown along the lake is one of the great drives in the country, with a series of signed viewpoints that are all worth stopping at. Bennett's Bluff in particular deserves more than a slow drive past.","It is also narrow with tight corners and considerable cyclist and tourist traffic. Allow well over an hour each way with stops, and take particular care in a large vehicle at the pull-offs, which are small.",["Allow over an hour each way","Bennett's Bluff is the stop to make","Pull-offs are small for campervans"]),s("Camping near Queenstown is constrained","Queenstown Lakes District enforces freedom camping restrictions strictly, with defined zones, certification requirements and early morning patrols. This is not a district where an unmarked lay-by is a reasonable gamble, and infringements here are common.","The DOC sites at Twelve Mile Delta and Moke Lake are the practical options close to town and fill early in summer. Both are worth arriving at in the early afternoon rather than at dusk."),s("Paradise and the Dart Valley","Beyond Glenorchy the road turns to gravel and continues towards Paradise and the Dart. It is passable in a normal vehicle in good conditions but includes fords that become impassable after rain, and many rental agreements prohibit it.","Check your agreement and the conditions before going, and turn around at any ford where you cannot see the bottom. The scenery is genuinely exceptional and it is not worth a recovery bill or worse.",["Gravel with fords beyond Glenorchy","Many rentals prohibit this road","Never cross a ford you cannot see into"]),s("Doing Queenstown without the adrenaline budget","The town is built around paid adventure activities and it is easy to spend several hundred dollars a day. It is equally possible to have an excellent few days on the Queenstown Hill and Ben Lomond walks, the lakefront, the Gardens and a swim, none of which cost anything.","If you want one paid activity, the Shotover jet and the gondola are the most reliably weather-independent. Skydiving and paragliding cancel frequently, so book them early in your stay."),{heading:"Walks from Glenorchy",body:["The Routeburn Track starts near Glenorchy and the first section to Routeburn Flats makes an excellent day walk without needing hut bookings. The full crossing is a Great Walk requiring booking well in advance and transport at both ends.","Lake Sylvan and the Rees-Dart valley tracks give quieter options with easier access, and the Glenorchy Walkway around the lagoon is flat, short and has the best views of the head of the lake for minimal effort.","The Routeburn road and the valley roads beyond Glenorchy are gravel and subject to closure and flooding. Check DOC alerts and your rental agreement, and be aware that the Routeburn car park fills early in summer."]},s("Weather and the lake","Lake Wakatipu generates its own wind and the afternoon breeze up the lake is reliable enough to plan around. Mornings are usually calmer, which matters for kayaking, photography and anything on the water.","Winter brings the ski fields and a completely different town, with the Crown Range requiring chains and Glenorchy road conditions worth checking before setting out.",["Mornings are calmest on the lake","Crown Range needs chains in winter"])]}),
  a({slug:"wanaka-haast-pass-route",title:"Wānaka to Haast Pass: a 3-day nature route",description:"Lakeshore bends, beech forest and powerful rivers across the Main Divide.",category:"Road trips",region:"Otago and Westland",places:["Wānaka","Lake Hāwea","Makarora","Blue Pools","Haast"],imageAlt:"Haast Pass road through native forest",intro:"Wānaka to Haast is a half-day transfer only if nothing is closed and nothing invites a walk. Three days creates room for Makarora, the pass waterfalls and a patient arrival on the wet western side.",sections:[s("Wānaka to Makarora","Leave Wānaka with fuel and groceries, then follow Lake Hāwea and the head of Lake Wānaka without racing the curves. Use signed lookouts, because roadside shoulders disappear quickly.","Makarora offers the natural overnight break and access to short walks or longer backcountry trips. Check sandflies, river conditions and accommodation hours before settling in."),s("Across Haast Pass","The pass road can close for snow, slips, fallen trees or crashes. Review Waka Kotahi updates immediately before departure and keep enough supplies to wait or turn back.","Waterfall and Blue Pools tracks change with maintenance and flood damage. Follow the current DOC notice and barriers rather than assuming a famous stop is open because vehicles are parked nearby."),s("Haast coast","The western side is wetter, greener and more exposed to sandflies. A roofed stay or powered site can be worth the money when several days of rain have soaked the vehicle.","Choose the next move only after checking the coast road north. Haast is a place to reset fuel, food and expectations, not merely the end of the pass."),s("Haast Pass is the lowest of the alpine crossings","At 563 metres it is the lowest road pass over the Southern Alps, which makes it the most reliable of the three in winter. It is still subject to closure from slips and snow, and the western descent is steep with tight corners and frequent one-lane bridges.","Check the NZTA status before crossing, particularly after heavy rain. When it closes there is no short alternative; the detour is back through Wānaka and around, which is most of a day.",["Lowest alpine pass at 563 metres","Steep western descent with tight corners","No short detour when it closes"]),s("The roadside walks are the point","Blue Pools, Fantail Falls, Thunder Creek Falls and the Haast Pass lookout are all short walks close to the road, and together they turn a two-hour drive into a satisfying day. Blue Pools is the standout, with a swing bridge and genuinely startling water clarity.","The Blue Pools bridge and track have been closed for reconstruction at various points, so check the current DOC status. Do not jump from the bridge; it is prohibited and people have been seriously injured doing it."),s("Wānaka is the calmer alternative to Queenstown","An hour from Queenstown with a similar lake and mountains, Wānaka has a fraction of the intensity and noticeably better value. Roys Peak is the famous walk and is a hard six hours return with a queue at the viewpoint in summer.","Diamond Lake and the Isthmus Peak track give comparable views with far fewer people. The lone tree in the lake is five minutes from town and busiest at sunrise, which is also when it is worth seeing.",["Roys Peak is six hard hours","Isthmus Peak has the same views, fewer people","Wānaka is better value than Queenstown"]),s("Sandflies begin at the pass","The eastern side around Wānaka and Hāwea is largely sandfly-free, and the moment you descend the western side that changes completely. Haast and the coast beyond it are serious sandfly country and the transition is abrupt.","Have repellent accessible in the cab rather than packed in the back before you start the descent. Stopping at Thunder Creek Falls without it is a memorable mistake."),{heading:"Lake Hāwea and the Neck",body:["The road runs along Lake Hāwea then crosses the narrow isthmus known as the Neck between the two lakes before following Lake Wānaka north. The viewpoint at the Neck looks down both lakes at once and is one of the better roadside stops in the country.","Hāwea is quieter than Wānaka with a good lakefront campground and considerably fewer people. It works well as a base if Wānaka is full or expensive, and it is only fifteen minutes further on.","Makarora, at the northern end, is the last settlement before the pass and has fuel, a cafe and camping. It is also the departure point for flights and jet boat trips into the Siberia Valley if you want a day out of the vehicle."]},s("Where to stop for the night","Camping options thin out considerably between Makarora and Haast, and the DOC sites along the pass are basic with no reception. Fill water and fuel at Makarora rather than assuming something appears.","Haast township itself has fuel, a shop and accommodation but very little else. Treat it as a service stop rather than a destination.",["Fill up at Makarora","Haast is a service stop, not a destination"])]}),
  a({slug:"southern-scenic-route-camping",title:"Southern Scenic Route camping guide",description:"Wild Catlins beaches, southern towns and Fiordland gateways between Dunedin and Queenstown.",category:"Road trips",region:"Southland and Otago",places:["Dunedin","Nugget Point","Curio Bay","Invercargill","Te Anau","Queenstown"],imageAlt:"Catlins coast on the Southern Scenic Route",intro:"The Southern Scenic Route links more than 600 kilometres of coast, forest and lake country. Seven days gives the Catlins two nights, keeps Fiordland from becoming a detour and leaves room for southern weather.",sections:[s("Dunedin through the Catlins","Stock up in Dunedin, then use Balclutha or Owaka as the practical entry to the Catlins. Waterfalls and beaches sit on side roads that turn a short map distance into a full afternoon.","At Nugget Point, Curio Bay and wildlife beaches, follow seasonal access and keep well away from sea lions, penguins and nesting birds. Darkness and wildlife make late coastal driving a poor trade."),s("Invercargill and the south","Invercargill provides repairs, groceries and indoor options after wet days. Bluff adds a satisfying geographic marker, but it should not replace time for local museums, food or a vehicle reset.","If travelling to Rakiura, park or book the vehicle according to ferry arrangements and expect Foveaux Strait weather to change schedules. Keep a flexible mainland night."),s("Te Anau to Queenstown","Use Te Anau for Fiordland rather than driving Milford Sound as a day trip from Queenstown. Fuel, road conditions and avalanche controls need checking before the early start.","Finish via the lakes to Queenstown with enough margin for traffic and roadworks. A shorter last day preserves the concentration needed after a week of narrow roads."),s("The Catlins are the reason to take this route","Between Balclutha and Invercargill the coastline holds Nugget Point, Purakaunui Falls, Cathedral Caves and Curio Bay, and it sees a fraction of the traffic of the main highway. The road includes unsealed sections and everything takes longer than the map suggests.","Give the Catlins two days rather than one. Compressed into a single transit it becomes a series of car park visits, which is exactly what the route is not for.",["Two days for the Catlins","Some sections are unsealed","Far quieter than the main highway"]),s("Cathedral Caves and Curio Bay run on the tide","Cathedral Caves are only accessible around two hours either side of low tide, the access road is privately managed with an entry fee and seasonal opening, and it closes entirely outside the season. Turning up without checking is the standard disappointment here.","Curio Bay's petrified forest is also tide-dependent, and the yellow-eyed penguins come ashore in the evening. Keep well back and behind the roped areas, because disturbance keeps them at sea and away from their chicks."),s("Wildlife here needs distance","Nugget Point has fur seals and sea lions, Curio Bay has hoiho, and sea lions haul out on beaches throughout the region. New Zealand sea lions are large, fast over short distances and will charge if you get between them and the water.","Twenty metres is the minimum for seals and sea lions, and considerably more for penguins coming ashore. Never position yourself between an animal and the sea, and keep dogs away entirely.",["Twenty metres minimum from sea lions","Never block the route to the water","Penguins will not land if you are visible"]),s("Weather from the south","This coast faces the Southern Ocean and gets weather straight off it. It can be genuinely cold and wind-driven at any time of year, and a warm Invercargill afternoon means nothing about conditions at Slope Point an hour later.","Carry warm and windproof layers regardless of the season, and choose sheltered campsites. An exposed pitch on this coast in a southerly is not a good night."),{heading:"The Otago Peninsula and Dunedin",body:["The peninsula east of Dunedin has the world's only mainland royal albatross colony at Taiaroa Head, along with sea lions, fur seals and yellow-eyed penguins. The albatross centre is a paid tour and the birds are visible year-round, with the best flying in windy conditions.","The peninsula road is narrow, winding and slow, hugging the harbour edge with almost no shoulder. It is not a comfortable drive in a large campervan and the high road over the ridge is the easier alternative in either direction.","Dunedin itself has the Otago Museum, Speight's brewery and Baldwin Street, and it is the last full-service city before Invercargill. Restock properly here, because the Catlins have very limited shops."]},s("Invercargill and Bluff","Invercargill is the last full-service city and the natural restock before Fiordland or the ferry to Rakiura. Bluff, half an hour south, is the signpost at the end of the country and the departure point for Stewart Island.","Bluff oysters are in season roughly March to August and are genuinely worth the detour when they are. Outside that, the town is quiet and the drive is mostly about having reached the end.",["Restock in Invercargill","Bluff oysters run March to August"])]}),
  a({slug:"fiordland-camping-road-trip",title:"Fiordland camping road trip from Te Anau",description:"Deep lakes, rain-fed waterfalls and remote roads built around a stable Te Anau base.",category:"Road trips",region:"Fiordland",places:["Te Anau","Manapouri","Milford Sound","Eglinton Valley","Kepler Track"],imageAlt:"Fiordland mountains reflected in dark water",intro:"Fiordland is easier when the campsite stays put. Base around Te Anau for four nights, then let weather and road conditions decide the order of Milford Sound, Manapouri and the walking days.",sections:[s("Build the base","Choose a Te Anau holiday park for services or an approved DOC campsite that matches the vehicle and water needs. Book summer dates early and never use a trailhead as an improvised overnight site.","Buy food and fuel before each long day. There are no ordinary fuel stations on the Milford Road, and reception is unreliable through much of the national park."),s("Milford Road","Check road status, avalanche controls and weather before leaving. Drive early, use designated stops and remember that the same beautiful waterfalls can signal intense rain and rising water.","A cruise booking does not protect against a closed road. Keep the operator informed, allow generous travel time and accept that Fiordland sometimes replaces the plan with weather."),s("Walking and recovery","Use the Kepler Track edges, lakefront and shorter DOC walks for flexible days. Longer tramps require hut bookings, backcountry equipment and a forecast suited to the full route.","Dry boots, refill water and rest between major outings. Fiordland's scale is best absorbed with spare time, not from a vehicle that returns to Te Anau exhausted every night."),s("The Milford road is a serious drive","State Highway 94 from Te Anau to Milford is 119 kilometres, takes at least two hours each way without stops, has no fuel, no reception for most of its length, and is subject to avalanche closure in winter and spring. It is also one of the best drives anywhere.","Leave early, fill up in Te Anau, and allow a full day for the return trip with stops at Mirror Lakes, the Chasm and the Eglinton Valley. Do not attempt it as an afternoon addition to another day.",["No fuel and no reception on the road","Two hours each way minimum","Full day for the return trip"]),s("Winter avalanche control closes the road","Between roughly May and November the Milford road operates under an avalanche risk programme, with scheduled closures and mandatory chain carriage at times. Closures can be announced at short notice and the road can shut with vehicles at Milford, requiring an overnight stay.","Check the status on the morning you travel, carry chains if required, and never drive past a closure barrier. The avalanche paths above that road are large and the control programme exists because of a genuine history of them."),s("Rain makes Milford better","Milford Sound receives around seven metres of rain a year and the waterfalls that make it extraordinary only run properly when it is raining. A clear day gives you the peaks; a wet day gives you hundreds of temporary falls down the cliff faces.","Book a cruise regardless of the forecast, take a waterproof layer and go outside on the deck. Travellers who cancel because of rain are cancelling the better version of the experience.",["Waterfalls need rain to run","Book the cruise regardless of forecast","Go out on deck, take waterproofs"]),s("Camping and the sandflies","There are DOC campsites through the Eglinton Valley along the road, which are the practical option since accommodation at Milford itself is very limited. Freedom camping near the fiords is not permitted, and the restriction is enforced.","Fiordland sandflies are the worst in the country and Milford's are legendary. Repellent is not optional here, and setting up camp in the Eglinton at dusk without it is genuinely unpleasant."),{heading:"Doubtful Sound and the alternative to Milford",body:["Doubtful Sound is larger, quieter and reached by a boat across Lake Manapouri followed by a bus over Wilmot Pass, which makes it a full day and considerably more expensive than Milford. In exchange you see almost no other vessels.","For travellers who have the time and budget, the overnight cruises on either sound are the version most people remember. They book out well ahead in summer and are weather-dependent like everything else here.","Manapouri itself is a quieter base than Te Anau with a good campground and the Kepler Track trailhead nearby. If Te Anau is full, it is twenty minutes away and usually has space."]},s("Te Anau as a base","Te Anau has the services, the DOC visitor centre for track and weather information, and the accommodation that Milford lacks. Almost everyone bases here and drives out, which is the sensible arrangement.","The Te Anau glow worm caves are a boat trip across the lake and work well as an evening activity, particularly on a day when the weather ruled out something else.",["Base in Te Anau, not Milford","DOC visitor centre for track info"])]}),
  a({slug:"nelson-abel-tasman-golden-bay",title:"Nelson, Abel Tasman and Golden Bay camping route",description:"Sunny coast, golden beaches and the slow climb over Tākaka Hill.",category:"Road trips",region:"Tasman",places:["Nelson","Māpua","Marahau","Tākaka","Pūponga"],imageAlt:"Golden beach in Abel Tasman National Park",intro:"Six days gives Nelson one night, Abel Tasman two and Golden Bay enough time to justify crossing Tākaka Hill. The route works best when water transport replaces some road kilometres and the final coast remains flexible.",sections:[s("Nelson to Marahau","Use Nelson for supplies and a city night, then pause in Māpua or Motueka before Marahau. Summer traffic is slow and camps near the national park book early.","Reserve water taxis, campsites and huts through the correct operator or DOC system. Tides affect several coastal track crossings, so the day's schedule should begin with the tide table."),s("Over Tākaka Hill","Fuel in Motueka and cross the hill in daylight. The road is steep, winding and tiring for large vehicles, with winter ice or storm damage possible.","Tākaka makes the practical base for food and weather decisions. Te Waikoropupū Springs has strict contact restrictions that protect its water, and every barrier should be treated as non-negotiable."),s("Golden Bay north","Pūponga and Farewell Spit sit farther from services than they appear. Join an authorised tour where access is restricted and avoid driving onto tidal sand.","Keep the final night near Tākaka if a ferry or flight follows. Crossing the hill and reaching Nelson or Picton under time pressure removes the calm that makes Golden Bay worth visiting."),s("Abel Tasman is a booking exercise","The coastal track campsites and huts are booked rather than turn-up, and they sell out for the summer season well in advance. Water taxis run from Marahau and Kaiteriteri and are the standard way to combine a walk with a return, which also has to be booked.","Decide the shape of your walk before you book anything: one-way with a taxi pickup, a day walk from Marahau, or a multi-day with hut or campsite nights. Each has different booking requirements and they interlock.",["Track sites and huts book out early","Water taxis need booking too","Decide the shape before booking"]),s("Tidal crossings are not optional timing","Two sections of the coastal track cross tidal estuaries at Awaroa and Torrent Bay, and they are passable only around low tide. Missing the window means a long wait or a high-tide alternative track, and people are caught out on this regularly.","Get the tide table when you book and plan the day around it rather than around distance. The estuary crossings dictate your start time, not your preference."),s("Tākaka Hill and Golden Bay","The road over Tākaka Hill is steep, winding and slow, and it is the only route into Golden Bay. It takes longer than the distance suggests and is a real workout in a campervan, but the bay on the other side is worth the effort.","Te Waikoropupū Springs are among the clearest freshwater springs measured anywhere and are culturally significant to Ngāti Tama and Te Ātiawa. The water is not to be entered, and the boardwalk keeps you off it.",["Tākaka Hill is the only way in","No entry to the Pupu Springs water","Allow well over the estimated time"]),s("Farewell Spit and the far north of the island","The spit is a wetland of international importance and vehicle access beyond the first section is restricted to a licensed tour operator. Wharariki Beach, reached by a twenty minute walk across farmland, is the more accessible highlight and frequently the better one.","Check the tide for Wharariki, because the arches and the seal pups in the rock pools are only reachable at lower water. The walk crosses private farmland, so keep to the track and close the gates."),{heading:"Nelson and the Great Taste Trail",body:["Nelson has the highest sunshine hours in the country and a substantial arts and food scene, and the Great Taste Trail is a mostly flat cycle route linking wineries, breweries and cafes between the city, Māpua and Motueka. Bike hire and shuttle services make a one-way day easy.","The Saturday market in Nelson is one of the better ones in the country and worth planning around. Māpua wharf, on the trail, has a cluster of good food and a passenger ferry across the estuary.","Nelson also works as the restock point before Golden Bay, since the shops beyond Tākaka are limited and everything has to cross the hill. Do the big shop here rather than in Motueka."]},s("When to go","Abel Tasman is at its best from December to March and is correspondingly full, with track sites booked months ahead. Shoulder season gives you the same coast with availability and cooler water.","Nelson has the highest sunshine hours in the country, so even outside summer the odds of good weather here are better than most of the South Island.",["Book December to March far ahead","Shoulder season is much easier"])]}),
  a({slug:"arthurs-pass-camping-route",title:"Arthur's Pass camping route from Christchurch to the West Coast",description:"Braided rivers, alpine forest and a high mountain road across the Southern Alps.",category:"Road trips",region:"Canterbury and West Coast",places:["Christchurch","Castle Hill","Arthur's Pass Village","Otira","Hokitika"],imageAlt:"Arthur's Pass highway beneath alpine peaks",intro:"State Highway 73 is a crossing that deserves two or three days. Castle Hill, Arthur's Pass Village and the Otira descent each reward daylight, while snow, wind and kea require more attention than the distance alone suggests.",sections:[s("Canterbury approach","Leave Christchurch after checking the pass forecast and road status. Stop at Castle Hill only in the formed parking area and respect cultural and conservation guidance among the limestone formations.","Lake Pearson and other camps have exact rules for vehicles, fires and facilities. Confirm the selected site before arrival and carry water suitable for a basic alpine night."),s("Arthur's Pass Village","Use the village as the walking base and visit the DOC centre for current track advice. Alpine routes range from short valley walks to serious mountaineering terrain, and names alone do not show the difference.","Kea are intelligent and destructive around unattended gear. Never feed them, secure loose objects and protect rubber or exposed fittings without harming the birds."),s("Otira to the coast","Descend cautiously through the viaduct and steep grades, using lower gears instead of riding the brakes. Ice, wind and heavy vehicles can change the character of the road quickly.","Reach Hokitika or Greymouth before dark for a full reset. If the pass closes, wait or use a properly planned alternative rather than searching for an unsealed shortcut."),s("The highest of the three alpine crossings","At 920 metres, Arthur's Pass is the highest and most weather-affected of the main alpine road crossings. The Otira Gorge section on the western side is steep with a viaduct, and snow, ice and high wind close it regularly in winter.","Check the NZTA status before you leave, carry chains in winter, and treat a closure as a reason to reroute rather than wait. The Lewis Pass and Haast Pass alternatives are both long detours.",["Highest main alpine crossing at 920 m","Otira Gorge is steep with a viaduct","Carry chains in winter"]),s("Kea will damage your vehicle","The kea at Arthur's Pass and Otira are intelligent, curious and destructive, and they will strip rubber seals, windscreen wipers, roof racks and anything soft on a parked vehicle. This is not a small risk; it is the normal outcome of leaving a van unattended in the car parks.","Never feed them, because human food is what has drawn them to car parks and roads where they are killed. Pack away anything loose, and expect them to open a bag you thought was secure."),s("The walks are short and steep","Devils Punchbowl Falls is an hour return and mostly steps. Avalanche Peak is a serious alpine day with genuine exposure and no shelter, and it should not be attempted in poor weather or without proper gear despite being a signposted track from the village.","Castle Hill, on the eastern approach, is an easy walk through limestone formations and works well as a break on the drive. It is also a significant site for Ngāi Tahu, so keep to the tracks and treat it accordingly.",["Avalanche Peak is a real alpine route","Devils Punchbowl is an hour of steps","Castle Hill is an easy roadside stop"]),s("Village services are minimal","Arthur's Pass village has a small store, a cafe and limited fuel, and it is not a place to rely on for a full restock. Fill up in Springfield on the eastern side or Kumara on the western side, and carry food for the crossing.","The DOC campsites near the village are basic and can be very cold, sitting at altitude even in summer. Bring warmer bedding than the Canterbury Plains would suggest."),{heading:"The TranzAlpine as an alternative",body:["The TranzAlpine train follows roughly the same corridor between Christchurch and Greymouth and is widely rated one of the best rail journeys anywhere. It works as a return day trip from Christchurch without a vehicle, or as a way to see the pass in weather that makes driving unpleasant.","If you are driving, the viewpoints at the Otira Viaduct lookout and the Bealey Valley give you the scale of the pass without a long walk. Both have parking and both are places where kea will investigate your vehicle.","Coming from the east, Castle Hill and the Cave Stream Scenic Reserve break up the drive. Cave Stream is a genuine cave walk-through requiring a torch, warm clothing and care with water levels, and it should not be attempted after rain."]},s("Where to break the crossing","Arthur's Pass village is the obvious stop but it is small, cold and often full. Springfield on the eastern side and Kumara or Hokitika on the western side both have more options and better services.","If the pass is closed or the forecast is poor, the Lewis Pass to the north is the alternative crossing and a good drive in its own right, with hot pools at Maruia along the way.",["Village is small and often full","Lewis Pass is the alternative crossing"])]}),
  a({slug:"banks-peninsula-camping-weekend",title:"Banks Peninsula camping weekend from Christchurch",description:"Volcanic harbours, steep bays and French-influenced Akaroa close to the city.",category:"Road trips",region:"Canterbury",places:["Christchurch","Lyttelton","Little River","Akaroa","Okains Bay"],imageAlt:"Akaroa Harbour and Banks Peninsula hills",intro:"Banks Peninsula is close enough for a weekend and slow enough to feel farther away. The best route chooses one harbour, one camp and one major walk instead of chasing every steep bay road.",sections:[s("Choose the harbour","Lyttelton and the inner harbour suit a gentle first day, while Akaroa makes the stronger base for the outer peninsula. Leave Christchurch before weekend traffic and expect cyclists on narrow roads.","Little River is the last easy pause before the summit road. Strong wind, fog and stock traffic can make a modest climb demanding in a high-sided campervan."),s("Akaroa base","Park the vehicle and explore Akaroa on foot or by harbour trip. Marine wildlife operators follow different schedules according to sea conditions, so retain a land-based walk as the backup.","Camp only at authorised sites around the harbour and bays. Council restrictions, private property and narrow access remove many places that look possible on a satellite image."),s("Outer bays","Okains Bay and other outer roads involve steep grades and limited turning room. Confirm vehicle suitability and campsite access before descending, because reversing a large rig uphill is not a backup plan.","Return before late Sunday traffic or add another night. A short geographic distance does not guarantee a quick journey across the peninsula."),s("The peninsula roads are steep and narrow","Banks Peninsula is an eroded volcano and the roads reflect that: steep gradients, tight hairpins and narrow shelf sections, particularly on the routes to Okains Bay, Le Bons Bay and over the Summit Road. They are sealed but demanding in a large vehicle.","The Summit Road is the scenic option and considerably slower than the direct route to Akaroa via Little River. Take the highway out and the Summit Road back if you want both without doubling the driving time.",["Steep hairpins and narrow shelf roads","Summit Road is slow but worth it","Highway out, Summit Road back"]),s("Akaroa and the Hector's dolphins","Akaroa Harbour is home to Hector's dolphins, the smallest and one of the rarest marine dolphins in the world, and the harbour cruises are among the more reliable wildlife encounters in the country. They are also weather-dependent and book out when cruise ships are in port.","Check whether a cruise ship is scheduled before planning your day. On those days the village is extremely busy and the harbour trips fill with ship passengers."),s("The bays are better than the town","Okains Bay, Le Bons Bay and Pigeon Bay are quieter than Akaroa, have basic campgrounds, and give you the peninsula the way it is worth experiencing. Okains Bay also has a well-regarded Māori and colonial museum that is worth the stop.","Facilities in the bays are limited and there is no supermarket outside Akaroa. Restock in Christchurch or Akaroa before heading over the hill to a bay for the night.",["Restock before heading to the bays","Okains Bay museum is worth a stop","Bays are quieter than Akaroa"]),s("An easy weekend from Christchurch","The whole peninsula is within ninety minutes of Christchurch, which makes it the standard local weekend and means the campgrounds fill on Friday evenings in summer and over long weekends. Booking ahead matters more than the short distance suggests.","It also works well as the first or last stop of a South Island trip, since it is close enough to the airport to fit either side of a flight without a long drive."),{heading:"Lyttelton and the harbour side",body:["Lyttelton, through the road tunnel from Christchurch, has recovered from the earthquakes into a good small port town with a Saturday market and a working harbour. The Bridle Path over the hill is a solid hour's walk with views over both the city and the harbour.","Governors Bay and Diamond Harbour on the southern side of Lyttelton Harbour are quieter than the Akaroa side and reachable by ferry from Lyttelton as a foot passenger, which avoids the winding road entirely.","The Port Hills between the city and the harbour have extensive walking and mountain biking, and the Summit Road along them gives the overview of the whole peninsula before you commit to driving down into it."]},s("Getting there and the tunnel","The Lyttelton road tunnel is the quick way to the harbour and is closed to vehicles carrying certain goods, with height limits that catch some campervans. The Evans Pass road over the hill is the alternative.","From Christchurch the whole peninsula is within ninety minutes, which makes it the standard local weekend and means Friday evening arrivals compete for sites.",["Tunnel has height and load limits","Friday evenings are competitive"])]}),
  a({slug:"south-island-two-week-road-trip",title:"South Island camping road trip: a realistic 14-day loop",description:"A two-week route that protects time for mountains, coast and Fiordland instead of collecting towns.",category:"Road trips",region:"South Island",places:["Christchurch","Lake Tekapo","Wānaka","Te Anau","West Coast","Arthur's Pass"],imageAlt:"South Island alpine road and campervan",intro:"Fourteen days is enough for a memorable Te Waipounamu loop when the itinerary accepts limits. This version chooses Mackenzie Country, Wānaka, Fiordland and the West Coast, with two flexible nights for weather.",sections:[s("Christchurch to Fiordland","Give Tekapo or Aoraki two nights, then travel through Wānaka to Te Anau without forcing both long walks and long drives into the same day. Queenstown can be a supply stop or a night, not an obligation.","Book the hard-to-replace nights first: Milford activities, peak DOC camps and any holiday-period stay. Leave ordinary town camps flexible around them."),s("West Coast north","Cross Haast Pass only after checking the road and forecast. Glacier country needs at least two nights if a flight or major walk matters, because cloud and rain frequently reorder the plan.","Hokitika or Greymouth provides the reset before Punakaiki and the alpine return. Use laundry, fuel and groceries deliberately rather than losing time to small errands every day."),s("Arthur's Pass return","Cross Arthur's Pass in daylight with snow chains when required and the driver's attention fresh. If the pass closes, accept the delay instead of stretching the final day into an unsafe detour.","Keep the last night near Christchurch for cleaning, refuelling and the rental handover. The spare margin also protects the trip when weather moves an earlier section."),s("A loop beats a line","Fourteen days is enough for a genuine South Island loop if you resist adding Stewart Island, the Catlins and Marlborough on top. A workable circuit is Christchurch, Tekapo, Aoraki, Wānaka, Queenstown, Te Anau, back up the West Coast and over Arthur's Pass.","That shape avoids backtracking, crosses the Alps twice by different passes, and leaves you finishing near where you started. Adding a second region usually means removing two nights from places you came for.",["Loop rather than out and back","Cross the Alps by two different passes","Adding regions costs you nights"]),s("Book the three things that sell out","Milford cruises, Aoraki accommodation and any Great Walk are the elements that will not be available late. Everything else on this loop can be arranged a few days out for most of the year.","Book those three first and build the driving around them. Trying to fit a Milford cruise into a route that was planned without it usually means an unpleasant day of driving at one end."),s("Weather will change the plan at least once","Over two weeks in the South Island you should expect at least one day where the pass is closed, the cruise is cancelled or the walk is unsafe. This is normal rather than bad luck, and an itinerary with no slack turns it into a crisis.","Build in two flexible days and hold a wet-weather alternative for each region. Te Papa is not an option here, but a hot pool, a museum, a brewery or simply a slower morning all are.",["Expect one disrupted day minimum","Keep two flexible days in the plan","Have a wet-weather option per region"]),s("Distances between fuel and food","The South Island has long stretches with no services, particularly on the West Coast, through Fiordland and over the passes. Treat Twizel, Wānaka, Te Anau, Hokitika and Greymouth as your restock points and fill up when you pass through rather than when you need to.","Reception disappears for hours at a time on several of these legs. Download the route, tell someone your plan and do not rely on being able to call ahead about a booking."),{heading:"What to leave out",body:["Two weeks does not comfortably include Stewart Island, the Catlins, Marlborough and Nelson on top of the main loop. Each of those adds two to three days properly done, and adding one usually means removing a night from Aoraki or Fiordland, which is a poor trade.","The most common regret is compressing the West Coast. It is the section with the longest distances between services, the most weather disruption and the scenery people remember, and it does not reward being rushed through in a day and a half.","If you must cut something from the standard loop, Queenstown is the easiest to shorten. It has the most expensive accommodation, the most crowding and the least that is unique to it compared with Wānaka and Te Anau nearby."]},s("Where to start and finish","Christchurch and Queenstown are the two main entry points and both work. Starting in Christchurch and finishing in Queenstown avoids backtracking, while a Christchurch round trip avoids a one-way vehicle fee.","Queenstown flights are more weather-affected than Christchurch, so if your departure is tight, finishing in Christchurch is the lower-risk option.",["Queenstown flights divert more often","Weigh one-way fees against backtracking"])]}),
  a({slug:"new-zealand-three-week-campervan-route",title:"New Zealand by campervan: a practical 3-week route",description:"Both islands with enough rest days, one ferry crossing and space for weather to change the order.",category:"Road trips",region:"New Zealand",places:["Auckland","Rotorua","Wellington","Picton","Aoraki / Mount Cook","Te Anau","Christchurch"],imageAlt:"Campervan beside a New Zealand lake",intro:"Three weeks can connect both main islands, but it cannot absorb every famous detour. This route keeps the north coherent, crosses Cook Strait once and concentrates the South Island on Mackenzie Country, Fiordland and the east coast.",sections:[s("Nine days in the north","Leave Auckland for Coromandel or the Bay of Plenty, then spend two nights around Rotorua. Continue through Taupō and Tongariro with a weather backup before reaching Wellington.","Use holiday parks for occasional laundry, power and long showers, then mix in suitable DOC or council sites. Freedom camping rules are local and vehicle-specific, not a general permission to stop anywhere."),s("Cook Strait and the south","Book the ferry for the correct vehicle length and height, arrive early and keep disruption margin. Picton deserves a recovery night after a rough crossing or late arrival.","Travel through Kaikōura and Christchurch toward Tekapo and Aoraki. Protect the alpine days from an overfilled schedule, because wind or rain may shift them by 24 hours."),s("Fiordland and finish","Use Wānaka or Queenstown as a transition, then base in Te Anau for Fiordland. Return to Christchurch through the southern route or central Otago only if the remaining days genuinely fit.","Plan the last 48 hours around the rental return, cleaning and fuel. A good three-week trip ends with a calm handover rather than a final overnight drive."),s("Three weeks is the first comfortable length","Twenty-one days is roughly the point where both islands become possible without the trip turning into a driving exercise. It allows around eight days in the North Island, one ferry day and twelve in the South, with two nights in the places that deserve them.","Resist the temptation to add Northland and Stewart Island at the ends. Either one converts a comfortable trip back into a rushed one, and both are better as their own journey.",["Roughly 8 north, 1 ferry, 12 south","Do not bolt on Northland or Rakiura","Two nights in the places that earn it"]),s("One ferry crossing, booked first","Cross Cook Strait once, southbound, and book it before anything else. A campervan is priced on length and height, summer sailings fill, and the crossing consumes most of a day once check-in and the drives at either end are counted.","Position the night before near the terminal and leave the day after soft. An evening arrival in Picton followed by a long drive is how people start their South Island leg tired."),s("Where to spend two nights","Rotorua, Aoraki or Tekapo, Te Anau and somewhere on the West Coast are the four places where a second night changes the trip most. Each is either weather-dependent or has more to do than a single afternoon allows.","Single nights work fine for transit stops like Taupō, Blenheim or Greymouth. The mistake is spreading single nights evenly across the whole route, which leaves you packing up every morning for three weeks.",["Two nights: Rotorua, Aoraki, Te Anau, West Coast","Single nights for transit towns","Packing up daily for 21 days is exhausting"]),s("Managing a campervan for three weeks","Over this length the vehicle routine matters: dump and refill on a regular schedule rather than when tanks are full, do laundry every five or six days, and plan a serviced night after each run of freedom camping. Letting these slip is what makes week three uncomfortable.","Keep the gas bottle, tyre pressures and windscreen washer topped up as you go. Small vehicle jobs are easy in a town and difficult on the Milford road."),{heading:"Direction of travel and one-way logistics",body:["Running north to south, picking up in Auckland and dropping in Christchurch or Queenstown, generally works better than the reverse because it puts the more demanding South Island driving at the end when you are used to the vehicle.","One-way rental fees between the islands vary widely and are sometimes waived in the low season when operators need vehicles repositioned. Ask directly rather than assuming the online quote is fixed, because it frequently is not.","The alternative is a round trip from Auckland, which means crossing Cook Strait twice and adds roughly two days of driving and a second ferry fare. It is only worth it if your flights are fixed to Auckland at both ends."]},s("Rest days are part of the plan","Three weeks of moving every day is exhausting, and the trips people remember well usually include two or three days where nobody drives anywhere. Build them in deliberately rather than hoping for them.","Choose places worth staying still in: a lakeside site, a town with good food, somewhere with a walk from the door. A rest day in a transit town is not a rest day.",["Plan two or three no-driving days","Rest somewhere worth staying"])]}),
  a({slug:"rakiura-stewart-island-camping-guide",title:"Rakiura / Stewart Island camping journey",description:"Ferry weather, forest tracks and bird-rich nights beyond the end of the southern road.",category:"Road trips",region:"Rakiura",places:["Invercargill","Bluff","Oban","Ulva Island","Rakiura Track"],imageAlt:"Remote beach and native forest on Rakiura",intro:"Rakiura is a trip without the campervan. Leave the vehicle on the mainland, cross with a compact pack and let walking, water taxis and island weather set the pace for three or four nights.",sections:[s("Mainland preparation","Buy specialist food and equipment in Invercargill, then confirm secure parking and check-in at Bluff. Ferry luggage rules and sea conditions deserve attention before the morning of departure.","Foveaux Strait can be rough and services may change. Keep essential medication, warm layers and a flexible mainland booking for any delayed crossing."),s("Oban and Ulva Island","Oban is the service base, not a large town. Book accommodation, camps or track huts in advance and carry what small shops may not stock.","Ulva Island is a predator-free sanctuary reached by water taxi. Clean footwear and bags, follow biosecurity instructions and keep food sealed so the visit does not introduce pests."),s("Tracks and night wildlife","The Rakiura Track requires the correct hut or campsite booking and gear for persistent mud and rain. Shorter Oban walks still need a torch, warm layer and realistic return time.","Kiwi viewing is quiet and patient. Use red light where guides request it, give birds space and never use food or sound to pull wildlife closer."),s("Getting there is a decision in itself","The ferry from Bluff takes about an hour across Foveaux Strait, which has a reputation for being rough that is entirely deserved. The alternative is a short flight from Invercargill, which costs more, carries a strict baggage limit and is weather-dependent.","You cannot take a vehicle. Leave it in secure parking at Bluff or Invercargill and plan to be on foot on the island, which has very few roads and where almost everything is within walking distance of Oban anyway.",["No vehicles on the ferry","Foveaux Strait is genuinely rough","Flights have strict baggage limits"]),s("Kiwi are actually visible here","Rakiura is the most reliable place in the country to see wild kiwi, and the Stewart Island tokoeka is active in daylight as well as at night, which is unusual. Guided evening trips to Ocean Beach have a high success rate.","Use a red light rather than white, keep quiet and stay on the track. Never approach or follow a bird, and keep well back if one crosses in front of you."),s("Ulva Island is the easy highlight","A short water taxi from Golden Bay wharf reaches Ulva Island, a predator-free open sanctuary where saddleback, robin, kākā and weka are abundant and unafraid. It is the single best few hours available on the island and needs no tramping experience.","Check your bag for rodents and seeds before boarding, which is why the checking stations exist. A single rat reaching Ulva would undo decades of work.",["Check your bag before boarding","Best birdlife you will see anywhere","No tramping experience needed"]),s("Weather, mud and the Rakiura Track","The Rakiura Track is a three-day Great Walk with huts and campsites that must be booked, and the North West Circuit beyond it is a serious multi-day route famous for deep mud. Neither should be underestimated because the island is small.","It rains on roughly 275 days a year. Waterproof boots, gaiters, dry bags and a genuine change of clothes are the difference between an enjoyable walk and a miserable one, and the mud is not an exaggeration."),{heading:"How long to stay",body:["Two nights is the minimum that justifies the crossing, and three or four is better. A single night gives you an evening and a morning, which is not enough for both a kiwi trip and Ulva Island, and leaves you hostage to the ferry schedule.","Oban is the only settlement and has a hotel, a shop, a couple of places to eat and limited accommodation, all of which book out in summer. There is a DOC campsite and backpacker options, and prices reflect that everything arrives by boat.","Bring cash and anything specific you need, because the shop is small and resupply is not quick. The island runs on a slower rhythm than the mainland and that is largely the point of going."]},s("Booking the crossing and the weather","Ferry sailings are cancelled in poor conditions and the strait is exposed. Build a spare day into the plan at each end rather than connecting the crossing tightly to a flight or another booking.","Take motion sickness medication before boarding rather than after. An hour on Foveaux Strait in a swell is a genuinely uncomfortable experience if you are prone to it.",["Build a spare day at each end","Take medication before boarding"])]}),
];
